/*
Detection Rule: Azure Entra ID Impossible Travel Login
Author: Bryan Jorge
MITRE ATT&CK: T1078.004 - Valid Accounts: Cloud Accounts
Severity: High
Description: Detects when a user account authenticates from two geographically
distant locations within a time window that makes physical travel impossible
(default: >500km apart within 60 minutes). This indicates either credential
theft with concurrent use, or use of anonymizing proxies and VPNs to mask
the attacker's true origin. Uses the Haversine formula to calculate
great-circle distance between consecutive sign-in geolocations.
*/

index=azure_aad sourcetype="azure:aad:signin"
    resultType=0

| eval user=properties.userPrincipalName
| eval ip=properties.ipAddress
| eval country=spath(properties.location, "countryOrRegion")
| eval city=spath(properties.location, "city")
| eval lat=spath(properties.location, "geoCoordinates.latitude")
| eval lon=spath(properties.location, "geoCoordinates.longitude")
| eval app=properties.appDisplayName
| eval client_app=properties.clientAppUsed
| eval mfa_method=spath(properties.authenticationDetails{0}, "authenticationMethod")
| eval conditional_access=properties.conditionalAccessStatus

/* Sort events per user by time to compare sequential logins */
| sort user, _time
| streamstats window=2 current=true
    values(ip)    AS login_ips,
    values(lat)   AS latitudes,
    values(lon)   AS longitudes,
    values(city)  AS cities,
    values(country) AS countries,
    min(_time) AS login_1_time,
    max(_time) AS login_2_time
    by user

| eval time_delta_min=round((login_2_time - login_1_time) / 60, 2)
| where mvcount(split(latitudes, ";"))=2 AND time_delta_min < 60 AND time_delta_min > 0

/* Haversine great-circle distance calculation */
| eval lat1=tonumber(mvindex(split(latitudes,";"),0))
| eval lat2=tonumber(mvindex(split(latitudes,";"),1))
| eval lon1=tonumber(mvindex(split(longitudes,";"),0))
| eval lon2=tonumber(mvindex(split(longitudes,";"),1))
| eval R=6371
| eval dlat=(lat2-lat1)*0.017453293
| eval dlon=(lon2-lon1)*0.017453293
| eval a=pow(sin(dlat/2),2) + cos(lat1*0.017453293)*cos(lat2*0.017453293)*pow(sin(dlon/2),2)
| eval distance_km=round(R*2*asin(sqrt(a)),0)

| where distance_km > 500

| eval speed_kmh=round(distance_km / (time_delta_min / 60), 0)

/* Score based on distance, speed, and authentication context */
| eval risk_score=0
| eval risk_score=if(distance_km > 5000 AND time_delta_min < 30,    risk_score+60, risk_score)
| eval risk_score=if(distance_km > 2000 AND time_delta_min < 30,    risk_score+45, risk_score)
| eval risk_score=if(distance_km > 1000,                            risk_score+30, risk_score)
| eval risk_score=if(distance_km > 500,                             risk_score+15, risk_score)
| eval risk_score=if(mfa_method="Password",                         risk_score+15, risk_score)
| eval risk_score=if(conditional_access="notApplied",               risk_score+10, risk_score)

| eval alert_title="Impossible Travel Detected: " . user
| table _time, alert_title, user, login_ips, countries, cities,
        distance_km, time_delta_min, speed_kmh, app,
        client_app, mfa_method, conditional_access, risk_score
| sort -risk_score

/*
TUNING PARAMETERS:
- distance_km threshold: Raise to 1000km in organizations with heavy VPN use
  where VPN exit nodes span multiple countries
- time_delta_min window: Widen to 120 to catch slower-moving attacker patterns
- mfa_method filter: Add | where mfa_method="Password" to focus on non-MFA sessions
- Whitelist examples:
  | where NOT match(login_ips, "^<vpn-gateway-ip1>|^<vpn-gateway-ip2>")
  | where NOT match(user, "svc-|automation@|sync@")

FALSE POSITIVES:
- Users connecting through corporate VPN with exit nodes in different countries
- Shared service accounts used by globally distributed teams
- Users switching between corporate VPN server locations between sessions
- Mobile users switching between cellular and WiFi networks with different geolocation

RECOMMENDED RESPONSE:
1. Contact the user immediately to verify whether they are traveling or using a VPN
2. Check Azure Identity Protection for correlated risky sign-in events on the same UPN
3. Identify which applications were accessed from each location
4. If unconfirmed: revoke all refresh tokens using Revoke-AzureADUserAllRefreshToken
5. Require the user to re-register MFA if the second factor may be compromised
6. Audit Exchange inbox rules and SharePoint permissions set during the anomalous session

INVESTIGATION QUERIES:
- Full sign-in history: index=azure_aad sourcetype="azure:aad:signin"
  properties.userPrincipalName=<user> earliest=-24h
  | table _time, properties.ipAddress, properties.location.countryOrRegion,
    properties.appDisplayName, resultType, properties.authenticationDetails{0}.authenticationMethod
- Inbox rules created: index=o365 Operation="New-InboxRule" UserId=<user> earliest=<first_seen>
  | table _time, Parameters
- OAuth consents granted: index=azure_aad sourcetype="azure:aad:audit"
  operationName="Consent to application" properties.initiatedBy.user.userPrincipalName=<user>

DATA SOURCES REQUIRED:
- Azure Entra ID Sign-In Logs (streamed via Diagnostic Settings to Event Hub → Splunk)
- Splunk Add-on for Microsoft Cloud Services configured for azure:aad:signin sourcetype

HIGH-RISK INDICATORS:
- Source IP resolves to a known proxy, VPN provider, or Tor exit node
- MFA method is Password only (no second factor, or legacy authentication)
- Conditional Access policy was bypassed or not applied
- Applications accessed include Exchange, SharePoint, Teams, or Azure portal

PREVENTION MEASURES:
- Deploy Conditional Access Named Locations policy to restrict access to trusted countries
- Enable Entra ID Identity Protection sign-in risk policy requiring MFA on risky logins
- Block legacy authentication protocols that bypass Conditional Access via policy
- Enforce FIDO2 or Certificate-Based Authentication for privileged accounts

RELATED TECHNIQUES:
- T1078 - Valid Accounts (parent technique)
- T1539 - Steal Web Session Cookie
- T1110.003 - Brute Force: Password Spraying

CHANGELOG:
v1.0 (2026-02-17): Initial release
*/
